{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "415",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 50s presented to the emergency department with 1 hour of acute, severe precordial chest pain radiating to his left arm, accompanied by sweating and nausea. Six hours prior, he had experienced similar chest pain at rest that resolved spontaneously after 30 minutes. Medical history included hypertension for 5 years, treated with reserpine.\n\nPatient had diaphoresis and was in moderate distress. Vital signs: blood pressure 170/109 mm Hg, heart rate 87 beats per minute, respiratory rate 20 breaths per minute, oxygen saturation 96% on room air. Jugular venous pressure was not elevated, lungs were clear. Heart sounds were regular and normal, without murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Other physical examination findings were unremarkable.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\nInitial serum troponin I level: 1.159 ng/mL (normal range ≤0.028 ng/mL)\n\n- ECG Findings:\nFirst ECG (Figure A):\n- Sinus rhythm of 67 beats per minute\n- Upsloping ST-segment depression leads V1 through V6\n- Tall, symmetric T waves in leads V2 and V3\n- 2-mm ST-segment elevation in avR\n- Reciprocal ST-segment depression in leads II, III, and aVF\n\nSecond ECG (Figure B):\n- Upsloping ST-segment depression in leads V3 through V6\n- Biphasic T waves in leads V2 to V4\n\n- Coronary Angiography:\nRevealed 95% stenosis of the proximal left anterior descending artery\n\n- Echocardiography:\n- Hypomotility in the anterior wall\n- Left ventricular ejection fraction: 57%\n- B-type natriuretic peptides were within normal range",
    "root": {
